{"name":"Fine Alabaster","hex":"#ecd3cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Senses referring to subjective quality.\nOf superior quality.","Of superior quality."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Well, nicely, in a positive, agreeable way.","Finely; elegantly; delicately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fine"},{"term":"alabaster","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine-grained white or lightly-tinted variety of gypsum, used ornamentally.","A variety of calcite, translucent and sometimes banded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of alabaster.","Resembling alabaster; white, pale, smooth, translucent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/alabaster"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alabaster","blocks":[{"text":"Alabaster is a soft rock used for carvings and as a source of plaster powder. Archaeologists, geologists, and the stone industry have different definitions for the word alabaster. In archaeology, the term alabaster includes objects and artefacts made from two different minerals: (i) the fine-grained, massive type of gypsum, and (ii) the fine-grained, banded type of calcite."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Lioness_Bast_cosmetic_jar_83d40m_tut_burial_artifact.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alabaster"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg/500px-Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg/500px-Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alabaster artefact: A composite bust of the E